# Help output

The brief help output should document CLI commands and options.

    :$ smth -h (exit: 0)

```stdout
switch to something else

Usage: smth [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  agent  Publish agent lifecycle state on the current tmux pane
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h                        Print brief help
      --help                Print complete help
      --config <PATH>       Path to a custom config file
  -b, --base <REPO>         Repository or workspace to use as the base context
  -B, --no-base             Force an empty repository context
  -o, --onto <REV>          Revision used as the base for newly created workspaces
  -q, --query <STR>         Seed the initial query
  -1, --select-1            Automatically switch when the initial query has only one match
  -0, --exit-0              Exit without opening the UI if the initial query has no matches
  -f, --filter              Filter non-interactively using the initial query
      --json                Print structured information about discovered entries
      --flag [<SESSION>]    Mark a live session as flagged
      --unflag [<SESSION>]  Clear a live session's flag
  -c, --create [<SESSION>]  Ensure a session exists without switching to it
  -s, --switch [<SESSION>]  Ensure a session exists and switch to it
  -x, --close [<SESSION>]   Close a live session without deleting its checkout
  -d, --delete <SESSION>    Delete a named workspace session and close it if live
  -r, --repo <GLOB>         Additional repository globs to surface alongside existing tmux sessions
  -V, --version             Print version
```

The agent command help should describe the exit action and every lifecycle state.

    :$ smth agent --help (exit: 0)

```stdout
Publish agent lifecycle state on the current tmux pane

Usage: smth agent [OPTIONS] <ACTION>

Arguments:
  <ACTION>
          Agent tracking action or lifecycle state

          Possible values:
          - idle:      The harness is ready, but no agent run has started
          - running:   The agent is actively processing a request
          - waiting:   The agent is blocked waiting for user input
          - succeeded: The most recent agent run completed successfully
          - failed:    The most recent agent run failed
          - exit:      The agent has exited

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help

      --title <TEXT>
          Optional one-shot title for a notification triggered by this transition

      --summary <TEXT>
          Optional one-shot summary for a notification triggered by this transition
```

The complete help output should include detailed key binding and configuration sections.

    :$ smth --help (exit: 0)

```stdout
switch to something else

Usage: smth [OPTIONS] [COMMAND]

Commands:
  agent  Publish agent lifecycle state on the current tmux pane
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h
          Print brief help

      --help
          Print complete help

      --config <PATH>
          Path to a custom config file. When omitted, smth reads $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/smth/smth.toml, or
          ~/.config/smth/smth.toml when $XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset.

  -b, --base <REPO>
          Repository or workspace to use as the base context. Named workspace paths use their
          default workspace when it is available, matching current-directory inference. When
          omitted, smth infers the base from the current working directory.

  -B, --no-base
          Force an empty repository context instead of inferring one from the current working
          directory.

  -o, --onto <REV>
          Revision used as the base for newly created workspaces. Defaults to trunk(). An explicit
          revision requires a repository base.

  -q, --query <STR>
          Seed the initial query

  -1, --select-1
          Automatically switch when the initial query has only one match

  -0, --exit-0
          Exit without opening the UI if the initial query has no matches

  -f, --filter
          Filter non-interactively using the initial query

      --json
          Print structured JSON information about discovered live sessions and repository
          candidates. The optional query narrows records using the same fuzzy matcher as the picker.

      --flag [<SESSION>]
          Mark a live session as flagged. The optional session name overrides a named workspace
          inferred from --base. The target must match the selected repository family and workspace
          identity.

      --unflag [<SESSION>]
          Clear a live session's flag. The optional session name overrides a named workspace
          inferred from --base. The target must match the selected repository family and workspace
          identity.

  -c, --create [<SESSION>]
          Ensure a session exists without switching to it. Existing repository checkouts receive a
          tmux session; missing named workspaces are created at --onto; plain sessions are created
          in the process working directory. Prints the actual tmux name.

  -s, --switch [<SESSION>]
          Ensure a session exists and switch the current tmux client to it. Creation follows
          --create semantics, and an existing live target opens its first window with a bell or
          agent attention.

  -x, --close [<SESSION>]
          Close a matching live tmux session without forgetting or removing any attached workspace.
          Repository-backed targets are verified against their normalized family and checkout
          metadata.

  -d, --delete <SESSION>
          Delete a matching named-workspace session from the selected repository family. The
          workspace is forgotten from jj, its checkout is removed, and the tmux session is closed
          when live, without an interactive confirmation prompt.

  -r, --repo <GLOB>
          Additional repository globs to surface alongside existing tmux sessions. Pass once per
          glob; these stack with repo.globs from config, and each matching jj repo can be used as
          context for new repo-backed workspaces. A leading ~ path component expands to the user's
          home directory.

  -V, --version
          Print version

Key bindings:
  C-d               Delete the repository and close the session.
  C-f               Flag or unflag a live session.
  C-n               Create the session without switching to it.
  C-o               Open or cancel the onto revision picker.
  C-p               Toggle the preview pane outside onto mode.
  C-r, M-r          Set or reset the current repo.
  C-u               Clear the filter.
  C-x               Close a live session.
  C-y               Confirm a pending deletion.
  up, down          Move selection by one row.
  C-k, C-j          Move selection by one row.
  M-up, M-down      Move selection to the first or last row.
  M-k, M-j          Move selection to the first or last row.
  S-up, S-down      Scroll the preview pane up or down.
  tab, S-tab        Jump between fuzzy matches in onto mode.
  enter             Accept the onto revision, or switch to the session, creating
                    it if necessary.
  esc, C-g, C-c     Cancel onto mode, or close the UI.

Configuration:
  The config file is TOML. It is optional; when no file is present, smth uses
  the built-in defaults.

  notification.bell  
                    Whether to emit a terminal bell in the agent pane when an
                    unfocused agent newly enters waiting, succeeded, or failed
                    state. Defaults to false. Bell failures never fail state
                    publication.
  notification.clear  
                    Optional command arguments run whenever an agent enters
                    running state. An empty command disables clearing. Failures
                    never fail state publication. Strings interpolate the {pane}
                    variable.
  notification.notify  
                    Optional command arguments run for the same agent
                    transitions as notification.bell. An empty command disables
                    command delivery. Delivery failures never fail state
                    publication. The root array executes directly. A nested
                    array is evaluated depth-first, POSIX-shell-joined, and
                    passed to its parent as one argument. Strings interpolate
                    {message}, {pane}, {socket}, {state}, {title}, and {tty}
                    variables.
  repo.globs        Glob patterns for jj repositories to surface alongside
                    existing tmux sessions. These stack with any --repo command-
                    line globs. A leading ~ path component expands to the user's
                    home directory.
  tmux.setup        Shell script to run after smth creates a detached tmux
                    session. The script runs in the new session's tmux context
                    and working directory, so commands can use default tmux
                    targets and relative paths.
  ui.sigil          Character used to mark live tmux sessions in the picker.

Example:
  Below is an example configuration that makes use of every available
  configuration field.

  [notification]
  bell = true
  clear = [
    "terminal-notifier",
    "-remove", "smth:{pane}",
  ]
  notify = [
    "terminal-notifier",
    "-title", "{title}",
    "-message", "{message}",
    "-group", "smth:{pane}",
    "-execute", [
      "/Users/me/.config/smth/focus-pane",
      "{socket}", "{tty}", "{pane}",
    ],
  ]

  [repo]
  globs = [
    "~/Code/*",
    "~/.config/nvim"
  ]

  [tmux]
  setup = '''
  tmux rename-window shell
  tmux new-window -n editor 'nvim .'
  '''

  [ui]
  sigil = "●"

```

